I wouldn't normally think a near-ascension was worth mentioning, but
one featuring a Lawful Monk might interest a few people. I've been
playing them as a challenge, needless to say. Lots of:

> Your armor is rather cumbersome...  You kill the invisible Wizard of Yendor!

I played 'Krism' the Student of Winds, and made it back up to the
Level One staircase, answered BY GOLLY YES to the 'are you sure you
want to climb' question, and then discovered that somewhere between
the 19th Wizard and Dungeon Level 1, I had put the Amulet down
somewhere. --

> Do you want your possessions identified?

Mmmm-Hmm. Probably I hit the wrong key when dumping a stack of useless
items in preparation for the Planes. A demon in Molech's lair DID say
to me -- in a strangely prophetic moment -- "Why search for the
Amulet?  Thou wouldst but lose it, cretin." In such situations "You
feel foolish. You obviously hadn't been paying attention."

For anyone who wants to walk the Path Of The Lawful Monk (just more
attentively) my tips are as follows:

(1) DO NOT fight the Wizard wearing body armor UNLESS you also have
>20 Dexterity.

You just can't hit him often enough. Everything else was fine, but I
had to level-teleport out of the first Wizard encounter for this
reason; one of the Wizards followed, but I was out of Gehennom then
and able to pray. I ditched the gauntlets of power for gauntlets of
dexterity, and then had no trouble, though I used up one or two
potions of full healing and an amulet of lifesaving in open-space
situations like Asmodeus' lair. Otherwise dragon scale is the bomb.
With a protection spell taking me down to -33 AC (with another wish or
some more Enchant Armor scrolls I could have maxxed at -37, I think),
the only serious threat is the Wizard's migraine attack. "Your head
suddenly aches" would do 30-40 HP of damage, so getting to the
stairwell in time was the name of the game.

(2) Reverse-genociding nurses is essential.

I gained an extra 150-200 HP from closing out the throne room of the
castle and making some cursed scrolls. Without that buffer, things
would have gone badly. Most wizard encounters took me down to within
200 of death (from about 400 max), some much closer.

(3) A ring of conflict is indispensable.

First for Master Kaen (Stay on the stairwell; the Xorns damage him so
much a monk with +7 Grayswandir can kill him in three hits), it's also
necessary later if you have to face two Wizards in an open area. It
all depends on getting them down into their keep-teleporting-away
pattern as quickly as possible. With conflict, and a regular diet of
trolls (you'll feel guilty but I only ate giants and trolls when they
were there, and my alignment was squeaky clean anytime I sacrified or
prayed). Otherwise, a ring of Increase Damage worked well, and Free
Action and Levitation were the only other ones I kept.

(4) Grayswandir and Excalibur work well.

I had 'Grand Master' in Martial Arts, but I used Grayswandir
throughout the game unless faced with a meaningful level drain attack,
which was rare. I had dipped for Excalibur and used it in the case of
wraiths; but I was killing them in one hit (almost) without fail by
the end, and nothing else managed a successful drain. I rec'd GS by
sacrificing, so, being Lawful, I 'escaped' the game with no artifact
wishes.

(5) Spell-casting is very useful.

I was switching to a robe for the first half of the game whenever I
needed to identify anything; having identify is fabulously useful; I
generally polypile Fort Ludios, but here I just did spellbooks and
wand in the main dungeon, then the drawbridge square at the castle (I
had all the gear I wanted). The most important spell was Extra
Healing, which I could cast without fail once I got to 'Basic' in
healing spells, followed by Protection, which gave a nice -5 AC boost
when needed (no Fort Ludious, and I hadn't played the Protection
Racket, so I never aquired any Divine Protection; In retrospect I
should have bought at least a couple of levels). I had a pet Angel at
one point using Charm Monster. I never got Finger of Death down below
a 96%  failure rate, so I can't comment on it. Even though I had Magic
Missile, I just used wands on the occasions that monsters got in a
line. Casting made me hungry, and I'd had the experience a few times
of killing mumaks in a single hit, so I didn't see that attack spells
would be any improvement on the basic hack n' slash.

(6) Wearing:

Amulet of Reflection
Eyes of the Overworld
+5 Helm of Brilliance
+4 Hawaiian Shirt
+5 Gray Dragon Scale Mail
+5 Gauntlets of Dexterity
+5 Water Walking Boots
+5 Cloak of Magic Resistance
Ring of Free Action (when required)
Oilskin Cloak (for Kraken)

-- Some of these were damaged and needed fixing.
-- Getting Silver Dragon Scale Mail would have freed up an amulet
slot.
-- I would have liked to find a ring of Slow Digestion somewhere, but
the pressure to keep eating giants and trolls is actually rather
healthy.
-- Speed boots would have given me slightly more attacks, but the WW
were +5 and I'd run out of ways to enchant things by the time I found
a pair of speed boots.

(7) One other point (should be obvious), keep a few blessed scrolls of
remove curse, and some holy water, in something other than a bag of
holding. Without MagicBane, you're inventory will just keep turning
red in Gehennom, and you'll certainly have to change a fair amount of
it back periodically.

